Use the matrix that gSPForceMatrix loads

On the N64, gSPForceMatrix gives the RSP a ready-made combined matrix (modelview x projection, in the N64's
fixed-point Mtx format) instead of letting the RSP multiply the two itself. In F3DEX2 it is two commands: a
G_MOVEMEM of the 64-byte Mtx to index G_MV_MATRIX, then a G_MOVEWORD G_MW_FORCEMTX that marks that matrix as
current. Vertices loaded after it are transformed by it. Fast3D ignored both commands, so anything placed this way
was drawn with whatever matrix came before. WCW/nWo Revenge places every 3D object like this and never sends G_MTX.

G_MV_MATRIX now decodes the Mtx into MP_matrix, the matrix GfxSpVertex transforms vertices with. The decode is the
one GfxSpMatrix already had, moved into DecodeMatrix so that both use it (its GBI_FLOATS memcpy now names the size
of the whole matrix, because sizeof of an array parameter is the size of a pointer). The modelview stack and the
projection are left alone, as on the N64: lighting and texture generation keep using the modelview, and the next
G_MTX or G_POPMTX rebuilds MP_matrix from the two. G_MW_FORCEMTX is accepted and needs no action, because Fast3D
multiplies the two matrices only inside GfxSpMatrix and GfxSpPopMatrix, so nothing later overwrites the loaded
one. The F3D/F3DEX form of gSPForceMatrix (four 16-byte G_MV_MATRIX_n loads) is still not handled.

Not in LibUltraShip main on 2026-11-08; drop this patch once it is there.

diff --git a/include/fast/interpreter.h b/include/fast/interpreter.h
index a7334a9a..f66cbf0e 100644
